<article class="env-lemma" id="0C4L">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0C4L">Lemma <span data-tag="0C4L">29.38.10</span>.</a> Let $g : Y \to S$ and $f : X \to Y$ be morphisms of schemes. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. If $\mathcal{L}$ is $g \circ f$-ample and $f$ is quasi-compact<a class="footnotemark" href="#a0000649362" id="a0000649362-mark"><sup>1</sup></a> then $\mathcal{L}$ is $f$-ample. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume $f$ is quasi-compact and $\mathcal{L}$ is $g \circ f$-ample. Let $U \subset S$ be an affine open and let $V \subset Y$ be an affine open with $g(V) \subset U$. Then $\mathcal{L}|_{(g \circ f)^{-1}(U)}$ is ample on $(g \circ f)^{-1}(U)$ by assumption. Since $f^{-1}(V) \subset (g \circ f)^{-1}(U)$ we see that $\mathcal{L}|_{f^{-1}(V)}$ is ample on $f^{-1}(V)$ by Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/0B3E" data-tag="0B3E">28.27.14</a>. Namely, $f^{-1}(V) \to (g \circ f)^{-1}(U)$ is a quasi-compact open immersion by Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/03GI" data-tag="03GI">26.21.14</a> as $(g \circ f)^{-1}(U)$ is separated (Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/09MP" data-tag="09MP">28.27.8</a>) and $f^{-1}(V)$ is quasi-compact (as $f$ is quasi-compact). Thus we conclude that $\mathcal{L}$ is $f$-ample by Lemma <a href="/tag/01VJ" data-tag="01VJ">29.38.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>